Fennell describes signaling a singular voltage source used to provide voltage to the working electrode, and guard electrode. Fennell describes the power source as coupled to an operational amplifier to provide different voltages to each of these elements. Fennell at    [0032]-[0034]. Specifically, Fennell discloses, "the work electrode 210 is operatively coupled to the inverting input terminal 404 of the operational amplifier 402, while the guard electrode 211 is operatively coupled to the non-inverting input terminal 405 of the operational amplifier 402. It can be further seen that the work voltage source Vw is provided to the non-inverting terminal 405." Fennell atSVG
{EMBED HERE}
0.150.077[0032]; FIG. 4A. Fennell requires that the guard electrode and working electrode maintain the same voltage. Fennell at   [0032]. Fennell is silent with respect to using separate voltage sources for the working electrode, the counter electrode, and the guard electrode. The instant claims, as amended, use separate voltage sources for each of these elements allowing the voltage for each to be varied from the others. Accordingly, Fennell cannot be relied upon to teach the limitations of the instant claims, as amended.
